{"answer":"There are 6 subsets: {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}—this list actually has 7 subsets, so the correct count is 7. One example is {1,8}. Exhaustiveness: a subset with four distinct positive elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10, so it has at most three elements. The two-element solutions are exactly the four complementary pairs summing to 9; checking each possible largest element for three-element solutions yields exactly {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify subsets by cardinality and enumerate all feasible one-, two-, and three-element cases.","id":"cardinality-enumeration","outcome":"candidate","reason":"Positive distinct elements force subsets summing to 9 to have at most three elements, making enumeration finite and complete."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in steps cardinality-bound through total-count.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"subset-count","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9.","step_ids":["cardinality-bound","two-element-list","three-element-list","total-count"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step example-subset.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["subset-count"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is one subset whose sum is 9.","step_ids":["example-subset"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"cardinality-bound","justification":"The least possible sum of four distinct positive elements is 1+2+3+4=10. Hence any subset with sum 9 has at most three elements. A one-element subset cannot sum to 9 because 9 is not in the stated ground set.","statement":"It suffices to consider subsets with two or three elements."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["cardinality-bound"],"id":"two-element-list","justification":"For a pair {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9, a can be 1, 2, 3, or 4; these give b=8, 7, 6, or 5, respectively. No other a is possible with a<b.","statement":"The two-element subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["cardinality-bound"],"id":"three-element-list","justification":"Let a<b<c and a+b+c=9. If a=1, then b+c=8 with b<c and b>1, giving (b,c)=(2,6) or (3,5). If a=2, then b+c=7 with b>2 and b<c, giving (b,c)=(3,4). If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12, impossible.","statement":"The three-element subsets are {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["two-element-list","three-element-list"],"id":"total-count","justification":"The two lists are disjoint by cardinality and contain 4 and 3 subsets, respectively; therefore their union has 4+3=7 subsets. The cardinality bound shows no remaining cases exist.","statement":"The exhaustive total is 7."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"example-subset","justification":"1+8=9, and both 1 and 8 belong to the stated ground set.","statement":"{1,8} is a valid example."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
